OpenAI just released GPT-6 Astra, and the launch was unlike any previous model drop. The company's president Greg Brockman said it marks the "start of the AGI era." Internal safety thresholds were triggered. Advanced cybersecurity capabilities are shipping gated behind a special program.

This isn't a incremental bump. Astra saturates math benchmarks, scores 100% on exploit testing, and can operate a computer end-to-end — filling forms, updating CRMs, building websites, and running QA checks without human intervention.

Here's what businesses need to know.

## What is GPT-6 Astra?

GPT-6 Astra is OpenAI's new flagship model, succeeding GPT-5.6 Sol. It launched September 3, 2026 as a limited preview for trusted partners, then rolled out to paid ChatGPT users the following day.

OpenAI calls it "the world's most intelligent and aligned model." The claim isn't just marketing hyperbole — Astra hits scores on reasoning benchmarks that were designed to stay ahead of AI for years.

The model is available today through:
- ChatGPT Plus, Pro, Business, and Enterprise
- OpenAI API as `gpt-6-astra`
- Microsoft Azure and AWS Bedrock

API pricing sits at $10/$50 per million input/output tokens. A faster "Fast mode" delivers up to 2x speed at 2x the standard price.

## The benchmarks that matter

Astra's headline numbers are striking. But not all benchmarks are created equal — here's what's real and what's harness-dependent.

### Reasoning and math

- **FrontierMath Tier 4 v2: 97.6%** — a research-grade math benchmark designed to be unsolvable. Claude Fable 5.1 scored 87.8%. Astra effectively saturates it.
- **ARC-AGI-3: 99.9%** — a visual puzzle test for abstract reasoning. But this score uses OpenAI's stateful "provider adapter harness." Stateless API calls score significantly lower (17–63% depending on reasoning tier).
- **GPQA Diamond: 96.0%** — graduate-level biology, chemistry, and physics questions.

### Computer use

This is where Astra gets practical for businesses.

- **OSWorld 2.0: 72.6%** at roughly 40 minutes per task, versus GPT-5.6 Sol's 65.7% at 75 minutes. That's a 47% cut in time per task — which directly translates to lower agent-running costs.
- **ScreenSpot-Pro: 92.7%** — grounding UI elements on screen without tools.
- **Agents' Last Exam: 59.3%** — complex professional tasks in real software (financial modeling, engineering, media production). Astra uses roughly 65% fewer output tokens than Claude Opus 5 here.

### Cybersecurity

Astra scores 100% on ExploitBench and 42.4% on ExploitGym. These numbers triggered OpenAI's internal "Critical" threshold under its Preparedness Framework. Advanced exploit-creation capabilities are gated behind OpenAI's Daybreak program — available only to vetted defensive security teams.

### Where it doesn't win

Astra isn't a clean sweep. On Humanity's Last Exam with tools, it scores 57.2% — behind Claude Fable 5.1's 65.0%. On FrontierCode 1.1 Main, it ties Claude Fable 5 (53.3% vs 53.5%). The competition is real.

## What's actually new for businesses

Benchmarks are abstract. Here's what Astra can do in practice.

### 1. Operate your computer end-to-end

Astra can fill out online forms, update customer records in a CRM, organize your calendar, and run frontend QA checks on a website you just built. It can install software, troubleshoot problems it sees on screen, and conduct online research — then draft summaries in your email or document editor.

This isn't a scripted automation. It sees the screen, interprets what's there, and takes action. For businesses, this means tasks that currently require a human afternoon can be delegated to an agent.

### 2. Produce finished professional artifacts

Astra is trained to follow your templates. It creates documents, presentations, spreadsheets, and analyses that match your writing and visual style — not generic markdown dumps that need reformatting.

OpenAI demonstrated Astra building a slide deck from a handful of template slides, keeping tone and layout consistent throughout. For any business that has spent hours reformatting LLM output into a corporate template, this is the capability that matters.

### 3. Ask focused questions instead of guessing

Earlier models either guessed (often wrong) or asked questions about everything. Astra fills routine gaps on its own and asks focused questions only when the answer could change the outcome.

In Codex, it can ask asynchronously — continuing work that doesn't depend on your reply while waiting for your input on consequential decisions. It stays oriented as tasks evolve, incorporating new requirements without dropping the original goal.

### 4. Keep notes across long sessions

GPT-6 Astra introduces a new way for Codex to preserve context when the context window fills. Instead of compressing long debugging sessions into a single summary (which drops details), Codex keeps searchable notes across windows. It can find a requirement or test result from an earlier message even if the note didn't capture it.

### 5. Build and render visually

With Sites in ChatGPT, Astra can create, host, and share websites, web apps, and games directly from a prompt. It brings stronger visual judgment to renderings — modeling a house in Blender and turning it into a walkable scene in Unreal Engine 5, for example.

## The "recurrent depth" concern

Astra uses a new reasoning technique called "recurrent depth" that obscures some or all of the model's chain of thought. OpenAI says this is a capability, not a bug — but AI safety experts have raised concerns about monitorability.

For businesses, the practical implication is: you may not always be able to see why Astra made a specific decision. For high-stakes workflows (financial transactions, legal decisions, medical advice), this is worth keeping in mind. Human-in-the-loop remains essential.

## How to start using GPT-6 Astra

Access is rolling out now. Here's the practical path:

1. **ChatGPT Plus/Pro users** — Astra is available in the model selector today.
2. **API developers** — Use the `gpt-6-astra` model ID. Start with the standard tier; switch to Fast mode only when latency matters more than cost.
3. **Enterprise** — Astra Pro is available for Business and Enterprise plans. Contact OpenAI sales for Daybreak access if you're in defensive cybersecurity.

For businesses evaluating how to integrate Astra into workflows, the key is to start with bounded, reversible tasks — form filling, research summarization, template-based document generation — before moving to autonomous agentic work.

## The bottom line

GPT-6 Astra is the most capable model OpenAI has publicly released. It's not AGI, and it doesn't beat every competitor on every benchmark. But its combination of computer use, professional artifact generation, and contextual judgment represents a genuine step change.

The businesses that benefit won't be the ones waiting for GPT-7. They'll be the ones learning to delegate real work to agents today.

